1
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,226
Welcome to part two.

2
00:00:06,226 --> 00:00:12,405
In part one you learned images, containers, ports, configuration and Dockerfiles.

3
00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:17,216
Now we make data survive, and we connect containers with networks.

4
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:22,834
We run a three container application, first by hand, then with Docker Compose.

5
00:00:22,834 --> 00:00:29,396
Then we operate it, secure it, make its image sixteen times smaller, and share it through a registry.

6
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:33,014
Finally, real troubleshooting, and the capstone:

7
00:00:33,014 --> 00:00:36,608
everything together, the way you would build it at work.

8
00:00:38,367 --> 00:00:41,043
Every container has its own writable layer.

9
00:00:41,043 --> 00:00:45,276
Write a file into it, and it lives exactly as long as the container.

10
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:48,218
docker rm, and the file is gone.

11
00:00:48,218 --> 00:00:52,763
That is fine for a web server, and a disaster for a database.

12
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:57,466
A volume is storage managed by Docker, outside every container.

13
00:00:57,466 --> 00:01:03,549
Mount it with dash v, name colon path, and any container that mounts it sees the same files.

14
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:06,355
Let's prove it.

15
00:01:06,355 --> 00:01:10,924
A container writes important data into a file, and reads it back.

16
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:14,169
Remove the container and start a new one:

17
00:01:14,169 --> 00:01:15,751
can't open data dot txt.

18
00:01:15,751 --> 00:01:17,926
The data died with the container.

19
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:19,661
Now with a volume.

20
00:01:19,661 --> 00:01:24,505
Create it, and let a container with dash dash rm write a message into it.

21
00:01:24,505 --> 00:01:27,756
That container is deleted the moment it finishes.

22
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:32,955
A brand new container mounts the same volume, and the message is still there.

23
00:01:32,955 --> 00:01:34,889
The data outlived its container.

24
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:38,090
Now a real database.

25
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:40,640
PostgreSQL 18, with a named volume.

26
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:44,210
We insert a row, delete the container completely,

27
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:48,800
start a new one on the same volume, and the row is still there:

28
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:49,893
I must survive.

29
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:57,614
And a trap that will catch you, because most tutorials on the internet are older than PostgreSQL 18.

30
00:01:57,614 --> 00:02:01,132
They mount the volume at var lib postgresql data.

31
00:02:01,132 --> 00:02:04,364
Version 18 refuses to start, with this error.

32
00:02:04,364 --> 00:02:07,954
The new place is var lib postgresql, one level up.

33
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:09,031
Read the error.

34
00:02:09,031 --> 00:02:10,898
It tells you exactly that.

35
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:16,831
A bind mount shows a folder of your computer inside a container.

36
00:02:16,831 --> 00:02:21,553
Here, nginx serves the site folder of the repository, mounted read only.

37
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:27,255
Edit the file on your computer, and the container serves the change immediately.

38
00:02:27,255 --> 00:02:27,964
No rebuild.

39
00:02:27,964 --> 00:02:30,477
That is the local development workflow.

40
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:32,867
Now a typo in the path:

41
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:34,462
sitee, with two e's.

42
00:02:34,462 --> 00:02:38,928
The container starts happily, and returns 403 Forbidden.

43
00:02:39,467 --> 00:02:40,292
Investigate:

44
00:02:40,292 --> 00:02:42,836
list the folder inside the container.

45
00:02:42,836 --> 00:02:43,661
It is empty.

46
00:02:43,661 --> 00:02:49,506
With dash v, Docker silently created an empty folder for the path that did not exist.

47
00:02:49,506 --> 00:02:54,182
Volumes are for data Docker manages; bind mounts are for your files.

48
00:02:54,182 --> 00:02:56,451
And always double check the path.

49
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:00,659
How do containers talk to each other?

50
00:03:00,659 --> 00:03:03,517
On Docker's default network, called bridge,

51
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:08,037
every container gets an IP address, but there is no name resolution.

52
00:03:08,037 --> 00:03:11,094
Ask for the name web, and you get bad address.

53
00:03:11,633 --> 00:03:16,234
Create your own network, and Docker runs a small DNS server for it.

54
00:03:16,234 --> 00:03:19,049
Every container name becomes a host name.

55
00:03:19,049 --> 00:03:24,061
That is how a web container finds its API, and an API finds its database.

56
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:30,470
And containers on different networks cannot reach each other, or even resolve each other's names.

57
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:33,374
That is isolation, and it is a security feature.

58
00:03:35,133 --> 00:03:36,007
Recorded.

59
00:03:36,007 --> 00:03:38,238
On the default network:

60
00:03:38,238 --> 00:03:39,985
wget, bad address.

61
00:03:40,533 --> 00:03:42,693
On our own network, labnet:

62
00:03:42,693 --> 00:03:46,293
the name web two resolves, and nginx answers.

63
00:03:46,833 --> 00:03:51,548
A container on another network, othernet, cannot even resolve web two.

64
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:58,095
Connect it to labnet as well, a container can be on several networks at once, and now it works.

65
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:03,047
Now the application this course is built around:

66
00:04:03,047 --> 00:04:04,453
a tiny message board.

67
00:04:04,453 --> 00:04:08,672
A web container with nginx, an API container written in Python,

68
00:04:08,672 --> 00:04:12,153
and a PostgreSQL database with its data on a volume.

69
00:04:12,153 --> 00:04:13,091
Four commands:

70
00:04:13,091 --> 00:04:17,443
a network, then the three containers, each with its own settings.

71
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:18,852
And it works:

72
00:04:18,852 --> 00:04:24,160
the API reports the database as healthy, and info shows which container answered.

73
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,212
Messages are stored in the database.

74
00:04:27,212 --> 00:04:29,794
It works, but look at those commands:

75
00:04:29,794 --> 00:04:33,492
long, easy to get wrong, and the start order matters.

76
00:04:33,492 --> 00:04:35,376
There must be a better way.

77
00:04:37,133 --> 00:04:41,554
Docker Compose describes the whole application in one file.

78
00:04:41,554 --> 00:04:44,551
Under services, one entry per container:

79
00:04:44,551 --> 00:04:45,750
web, api and db.

80
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:51,017
Each service either builds an image from a folder, or uses an existing image.

81
00:04:51,017 --> 00:04:53,590
Ports and the start order with depends on.

82
00:04:54,133 --> 00:04:57,168
Environment variables, exactly like dash e.

83
00:04:57,168 --> 00:05:02,532
And the service name db is also its DNS name on the network Compose creates.

84
00:05:03,067 --> 00:05:08,151
The named volume is declared once, at the bottom, and mounted by the database.

85
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,176
And then one command starts everything:

86
00:05:11,176 --> 00:05:13,779
docker compose up dash d dash dash build.

87
00:05:15,533 --> 00:05:16,789
docker compose ps:

88
00:05:16,789 --> 00:05:22,718
three containers, named after the project, which is the folder name, and the service.

89
00:05:23,267 --> 00:05:27,806
Compose also created a network, multi container app underscore default,

90
00:05:27,806 --> 00:05:30,811
and the volume, with the project name in front.

91
00:05:31,367 --> 00:05:37,421
And the application works, exactly like the hand made version, from one file you can commit to Git.

92
00:05:39,167 --> 00:05:43,816
docker compose down removes the containers and the network, and keeps the volume.

93
00:05:44,367 --> 00:05:49,651
So after up again, our message, written in chapter six, is still there.

94
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:54,029
docker compose down dash v also deletes the volumes.

95
00:05:54,029 --> 00:05:56,018
The data is gone, for good.

96
00:05:56,018 --> 00:05:56,901
Two letters.

97
00:05:56,901 --> 00:06:00,805
Use them only when you really want an empty database.

98
00:06:02,567 --> 00:06:05,951
Operating containers means knowing what they use.

99
00:06:05,951 --> 00:06:10,302
docker stats shows CPU, memory, network and disk per container.

100
00:06:10,302 --> 00:06:17,416
This one was started with dash dash memory 256 megabytes and half a CPU, and the limit column shows it.

101
00:06:17,967 --> 00:06:25,356
Now a Python program that wants 256 megabytes, in a container limited to 64.

102
00:06:25,356 --> 00:06:26,619
It is killed:

103
00:06:26,619 --> 00:06:27,786
Exited, 137.

104
00:06:28,333 --> 00:06:30,450
docker inspect confirms it:

105
00:06:30,450 --> 00:06:31,626
OOMKilled true.

106
00:06:31,626 --> 00:06:37,818
137 means killed, and OOMKilled means by the kernel, for using too much memory.

107
00:06:37,818 --> 00:06:42,443
When you see 137 at work, this is the first thing to check.

108
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:45,423
Security basics.

109
00:06:45,423 --> 00:06:48,251
By default, a container runs as root.

110
00:06:48,251 --> 00:06:52,685
Our final Dockerfile adds a user, and USER switches to it:

111
00:06:52,685 --> 00:06:56,125
id shows user ten thousand and one, not root.

112
00:06:56,667 --> 00:06:59,331
So the app cannot change system files:

113
00:06:59,331 --> 00:07:00,594
permission denied.

114
00:07:00,594 --> 00:07:04,731
With dash dash read only, it cannot change any file at all.

115
00:07:05,267 --> 00:07:10,126
Dash dash cap drop ALL removes the special powers root still has inside a container.

116
00:07:10,667 --> 00:07:13,107
And remember the password from part one?

117
00:07:13,107 --> 00:07:17,621
An environment variable is visible to everyone who can run docker inspect.

118
00:07:17,621 --> 00:07:23,355
The capstone passes the database password as a secret file instead, so it never appears there.

119
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:29,493
Same application, same code, three Dockerfiles.

120
00:07:29,493 --> 00:07:31,923
The typical first attempt:

121
00:07:31,923 --> 00:07:33,324
1.75 gigabytes.

122
00:07:33,867 --> 00:07:35,485
docker history shows why:

123
00:07:35,485 --> 00:07:40,081
the full Python image, plus compilers and tools installed just in case.

124
00:07:40,633 --> 00:07:45,572
The slim base with a cache friendly order and no pip cache:

125
00:07:45,572 --> 00:07:46,744
212 megabytes.

126
00:07:46,744 --> 00:07:47,498
Same app.

127
00:07:48,033 --> 00:07:49,587
And a multi stage build:

128
00:07:49,587 --> 00:07:55,996
stage one has the build tools, stage two copies only the finished result into a small Alpine image.

129
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:59,815
108 megabytes, sixteen times smaller than where we started.

130
00:07:59,815 --> 00:08:06,418
Smaller images download faster, start faster, and contain less software that can have vulnerabilities.

131
00:08:08,167 --> 00:08:10,750
Images are shared through registries.

132
00:08:10,750 --> 00:08:13,963
To practise without any account, run your own:

133
00:08:13,963 --> 00:08:16,267
the registry image, on port 5000.

134
00:08:16,267 --> 00:08:20,736
Tag the image with the registry's address in its name, and push.

135
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:23,694
The registry now lists simple app.

136
00:08:24,233 --> 00:08:30,176
Delete the local copy, run it again, and Docker pulls it back from the registry.

137
00:08:30,176 --> 00:08:32,256
Build, tag, push, pull, run:

138
00:08:32,256 --> 00:08:36,713
that is exactly how images travel from a laptop to a server.

139
00:08:37,267 --> 00:08:41,779
Docker Hub works the same way, with your user name in front of the image name.

140
00:08:41,779 --> 00:08:44,961
The capstone images of this course are published there,

141
00:08:44,961 --> 00:08:48,201
for Intel and for ARM computers like Apple Silicon Macs.

142
00:08:49,967 --> 00:08:51,870
Here they are on Docker Hub:

143
00:08:51,870 --> 00:08:54,861
docker from zero web and API, version 1.0.0,

144
00:08:54,861 --> 00:09:00,639
with a description that tells you how to run the capstone straight from these images.

145
00:09:00,639 --> 00:09:04,786
Anyone, anywhere, can now pull and run exactly what we built.

146
00:09:04,786 --> 00:09:07,301
Docker Hub is free for public images.

147
00:09:09,067 --> 00:09:12,358
Now the most important skill in this course:

148
00:09:12,358 --> 00:09:13,555
troubleshooting.

149
00:09:13,555 --> 00:09:14,527
Not guessing.

150
00:09:14,527 --> 00:09:17,295
The repository has ten broken setups.

151
00:09:17,295 --> 00:09:20,960
We will solve three of them, always the same way.

152
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,082
First, observe:

153
00:09:22,082 --> 00:09:24,176
what exactly is the symptom?

154
00:09:24,733 --> 00:09:26,563
Second, investigate.

155
00:09:26,563 --> 00:09:32,051
Collect evidence with docker ps dash a, logs, inspect, exec.

156
00:09:32,051 --> 00:09:34,338
Before changing anything.

157
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:36,370
Third, the root cause:

158
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:39,644
one sentence that explains every symptom you saw.

159
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:43,644
Fourth, fix it, by changing one thing.

160
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,732
And fifth, verify:

161
00:09:45,732 --> 00:09:48,456
run the check that failed again.

162
00:09:48,456 --> 00:09:50,414
Not it should work now.

163
00:09:50,414 --> 00:09:50,924
Proof.

164
00:09:52,667 --> 00:09:53,737
Scenario one.

165
00:09:53,737 --> 00:09:55,137
A colleague says:

166
00:09:55,137 --> 00:09:57,525
the container does not start.

167
00:09:57,525 --> 00:09:58,924
docker ps dash a:

168
00:09:58,924 --> 00:10:02,465
Exited with code 2, seconds after starting.

169
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,744
The logs say it directly:

170
00:10:04,744 --> 00:10:07,534
python cannot open file app main dot py.

171
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:09,975
What is actually in app?

172
00:10:09,975 --> 00:10:10,600
List it:

173
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:11,459
app dot py.

174
00:10:11,459 --> 00:10:14,740
And the Dockerfile's CMD runs main dot py.

175
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:15,599
Root cause:

176
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:19,114
the command names a file that does not exist.

177
00:10:19,667 --> 00:10:24,721
Fix the CMD, rebuild, run, and verify with curl.

178
00:10:24,721 --> 00:10:26,616
Fixed, and proven.

179
00:10:28,367 --> 00:10:29,916
Scenario three.

180
00:10:29,916 --> 00:10:32,911
Nothing answers on port 8080.

181
00:10:33,467 --> 00:10:34,832
docker compose ps:

182
00:10:34,832 --> 00:10:37,638
the web container Exited with code 1.

183
00:10:37,638 --> 00:10:38,321
Its logs:

184
00:10:38,321 --> 00:10:40,672
host not found in upstream api.

185
00:10:40,672 --> 00:10:43,478
nginx could not find the API by name.

186
00:10:44,033 --> 00:10:46,179
Which networks are they on?

187
00:10:46,179 --> 00:10:48,086
web is only on frontend.

188
00:10:48,086 --> 00:10:49,913
api is only on backend.

189
00:10:49,913 --> 00:10:54,840
They share no network, so the name api does not exist for web.

190
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:55,476
The fix:

191
00:10:55,476 --> 00:11:00,323
put the API on both networks, exactly like the capstone does.

192
00:11:02,067 --> 00:11:03,232
Scenario six.

193
00:11:03,232 --> 00:11:08,611
The page loads, but every API call returns 502, bad gateway:

194
00:11:08,611 --> 00:11:12,466
nginx is up, but nothing behind it answers.

195
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,605
The api container Exited with code 3.

196
00:11:15,605 --> 00:11:17,577
Its logs say it in one line:

197
00:11:17,577 --> 00:11:20,393
required setting DB_PASSWORD is missing.

198
00:11:20,393 --> 00:11:24,829
The application was written to fail fast, with a clear message.

199
00:11:24,829 --> 00:11:26,589
Write yours the same way.

200
00:11:27,133 --> 00:11:31,920
docker inspect lists the variables the container really got:

201
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:33,516
host, name and user.

202
00:11:33,516 --> 00:11:34,473
No password.

203
00:11:34,473 --> 00:11:36,787
Add it, recreate, and verify.

204
00:11:36,787 --> 00:11:41,095
Three scenarios, three root causes, and not one guess.

205
00:11:42,833 --> 00:11:48,250
The capstone is the same message board, built the way you would build it at work.

206
00:11:48,250 --> 00:11:51,593
Only the web container is published, on port 8080.

207
00:11:52,133 --> 00:11:54,927
web and api share a frontend network.

208
00:11:54,927 --> 00:11:58,324
Both run as normal users, with health checks.

209
00:11:58,324 --> 00:12:03,458
The API's filesystem is read only, and it has memory and CPU limits.

210
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,419
The database is on a separate backend network, with the API, and its password comes from a secret file.

211
00:12:10,967 --> 00:12:13,401
Its data lives on a named volume.

212
00:12:13,967 --> 00:12:19,781
And because web and db share no network, the web container cannot even resolve the database's name.

213
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:22,264
One command:

214
00:12:22,264 --> 00:12:23,786
docker compose up dash d.

215
00:12:23,786 --> 00:12:29,875
The database and the API are already healthy, and the web container's health check is just starting.

216
00:12:29,875 --> 00:12:34,138
Compose waited for each health check before starting the next service.

217
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:39,063
From the web container, the database is a bad address.

218
00:12:39,063 --> 00:12:40,680
Isolation, verified.

219
00:12:41,233 --> 00:12:44,707
The API runs as user app with a read only filesystem,

220
00:12:44,707 --> 00:12:48,639
and its database setting is a password file, not a password.

221
00:12:48,639 --> 00:12:50,933
docker inspect has nothing to leak.

222
00:12:52,667 --> 00:12:54,369
And here it is in a browser.

223
00:12:54,369 --> 00:12:59,538
The page comes from the web container, the info line from one of the API's processes,

224
00:12:59,538 --> 00:13:03,855
which shows the container's short ID, and the messages from PostgreSQL.

225
00:13:03,855 --> 00:13:06,835
These four messages survived docker compose down,

226
00:13:06,835 --> 00:13:11,091
when not a single container was left, because they live on the volume.

227
00:13:12,833 --> 00:13:14,189
And then we prove it.

228
00:13:14,189 --> 00:13:19,675
verify dot sh runs eighteen checks, the way an engineer would check a system by hand:

229
00:13:19,675 --> 00:13:22,773
containers healthy, only the web port published,

230
00:13:22,773 --> 00:13:25,484
the application end to end, both networks,

231
00:13:25,484 --> 00:13:28,131
non root users, the read only filesystem,

232
00:13:28,131 --> 00:13:31,293
no password in the environment, the memory limit,

233
00:13:31,293 --> 00:13:36,328
and finally docker compose down and up again, to prove that the data survived.

234
00:13:36,328 --> 00:13:37,490
All checks passed.

235
00:13:39,233 --> 00:13:40,772
One last break.

236
00:13:40,772 --> 00:13:42,618
Stop the database.

237
00:13:43,167 --> 00:13:51,576
The API still answers, but with status error and HTTP 503, and docker compose ps shows the database Exited.

238
00:13:51,576 --> 00:13:52,441
Root cause:

239
00:13:52,441 --> 00:13:54,641
the database is not running.

240
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:56,725
Start it, and verify:

241
00:13:56,725 --> 00:13:57,596
database ok.

242
00:13:57,596 --> 00:14:03,549
The messages are still there, because they live on the volume, not in a container.

243
00:14:05,300 --> 00:14:07,093
Docker uses disk space:

244
00:14:07,093 --> 00:14:09,354
images, build cache, volumes.

245
00:14:09,354 --> 00:14:14,344
docker system df shows how much, and how much you could reclaim.

246
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:18,389
And here is the command you will find in many blog posts:

247
00:14:18,389 --> 00:14:21,144
docker system prune dash a dash dash volumes.

248
00:14:21,144 --> 00:14:26,836
It deletes every unused image, all build cache, and every volume no container uses right now.

249
00:14:26,836 --> 00:14:30,386
Including the database of a project you stopped yesterday.

250
00:14:30,933 --> 00:14:31,129
So:

251
00:14:31,129 --> 00:14:38,231
look first with docker system df, prune with filters, and treat anything with volumes in it as deleting data.

252
00:14:38,231 --> 00:14:41,098
The cleanup lab explains each prune command.

253
00:14:42,833 --> 00:14:43,892
Let's look back.

254
00:14:43,892 --> 00:14:50,440
You can run, inspect and debug containers, and configure them with ports and environment variables.

255
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,602
You can write Dockerfiles that build fast,

256
00:14:53,602 --> 00:14:57,999
stay small and contain no secrets, and share images through a registry.

257
00:14:58,533 --> 00:15:03,167
You can keep data with volumes, connect containers with networks and isolate them,

258
00:15:03,167 --> 00:15:09,158
run a multi container application with Compose, limit and secure it, and troubleshoot it without guessing.

259
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:11,004
Now it is your turn.

260
00:15:11,004 --> 00:15:13,091
Everything is in the repository:

261
00:15:13,091 --> 00:15:20,133
eighteen labs, nineteen challenges, ten troubleshooting scenarios, the capstone, and a study guide as a PDF.

262
00:15:20,133 --> 00:15:24,437
Clone it, type every command yourself, break things, and fix them.

263
00:15:24,437 --> 00:15:28,806
When you have ticked the knowledge checklist, you can honestly say:

264
00:15:28,806 --> 00:15:35,131
I understand Docker, because I actually used it, broke it, fixed it, and built something with it.

265
00:15:35,131 --> 00:15:36,435
Thanks for watching.
